--- Probe 4: Source code analysis ---

File: plugins/huaweicloud-core/src/tools.mjs (lines 970-977)
Function: persistCredentials(ak, sk, securityToken, region)
  if (String(securityToken || '')) {
    return {
      status: 'error',
      error: 'Temporary STS credentials cannot be persisted (R3). Use action=temporary.',
      scope: 'rejected',
    };
  }
  // If STS is empty, proceeds to write with securityToken: '' explicitly
  writeGlobalCredentials({ ak, sk: String(sk), securityToken: '', region, configuredBySession: true });

ANALYSIS: R3 guard explicitly rejects any non-empty securityToken when action=persist.
The error message references R3 directly. Even after the guard, securityToken is forced to ''.

File: plugins/huaweicloud-core/src/auth/credentials.mjs (lines 236-261)
  let runtimeCredentials = null;  // module-level variable, in-memory only

  export function setRuntimeCredentials(ak, sk, securityToken, region) {
    runtimeCredentials = { ak, sk, securityToken: securityToken || '', region: region || '' };
  }
  // No writeFileSync → never persists to disk

ANALYSIS: action=temporary uses setRuntimeCredentials which stores in a module-level
variable. No file I/O. Token is lost on process restart.

File: plugins/huaweicloud-core/src/tools.mjs (lines 1146-1152)
  if (action === 'temporary') {
    setRuntimeCredentials(ak, sk, securityToken || undefined, region);
    return { status: 'ok', scope: 'temporary', note: 'Runtime credentials active...' };
  }

ANALYSIS: action=temporary path correctly routes to in-memory storage.

--- Conclusion ---
STATUS: PASS

R3 is properly enforced:
1. action=persist with STS token → explicitly rejected with R3 error
2. action=temporary with STS token → kept in memory only (runtimeCredentials variable)
3. credentials.json on disk has securityToken="" (verified)
4. KooCLI config has securityToken="" (verified)
5. No code path writes a non-empty securityToken to any file
